#X text 280 10 [nn.plot2d] - see what a 2-input network decides \, everywhere at once, f 120;
#X text 280 31 A map of the plane: x1 runs left to right \, x2 bottom to top \, both from -0.5 to 1.5. The four framed squares sit on the corners (0 \, 0) (0 \, 1) (1 \, 0) (1 \, 1) - the inputs of logic problems. Every cell is coloured by the network answer y at the centre of the cell: blue = 0 \, orange = 1 \, in between for answers in between., f 120;
#X text 280 82 HOW TO USE (a "callback" loop): bang the left inlet. For each of the 144 cells [nn.plot2d] sends the list "x1 x2" out of its outlet. Compute y in your patch and send it straight back into the RIGHT inlet - synchronously \, before anything else happens. Message "targets a b c d" colours the corner squares (1 = black \, 0 = white) for the 4 examples (0 \, 0) (0 \, 1) (1 \, 0) (1 \, 1)., f 120;
#X obj 280 158 inlet;
#X obj 780 158 inlet;
